  "OverallSummary": "This case involves Stewart Ginn's excessive trading and churning in five customer accounts between July 2020 and December 2022, resulting in over $2.22 million in realized losses while generating more than $2.24 million in commissions. For Customers 1-4 (retail customers), Ginn's actions constituted violations of Regulation BI's Care Obligation through excessive trading. For Customer 5 (a profit sharing plan), Ginn violated FINRA Rules 2111 and 2010 for quantitative unsuitability. \n\nThe excessive trading violations were established through several analytical steps: (1) Examination of cost-to-equity ratios, which ranged from 13.7% to 27.1% annualized, making profitability virtually impossible; (2) Analysis of trading patterns showing frequent in-and-out trading with minimal price changes but substantial commission costs; (3) Assessment of customer profiles showing three senior investors (ages 79, 88, and 72) and customers with moderate risk tolerances; (4) Documentation of Ginn's control over the accounts, including unauthorized discretionary trading; and (5) Evidence that Ginn placed his financial interests ahead of customers' by charging high commissions (generally 3% on purchases and 2% on sales) without discussing costs with customers.\n\nGinn also violated FINRA Rules 3260 and 2010 by exercising discretion without written authorization in four accounts. The customers had little trading experience and previously maintained buy-and-hold strategies with their prior representative. Three customers were retired seniors, including one with Alzheimer's disease. The case demonstrates a systematic pattern of abusive trading practices designed to generate commissions rather than serve customer interests."
